Functions as values.

For most of this course, the thing you pass around has been data — numbers, objects, collections. This module adds a second kind of value: behaviour itself. A piece of code — "how to compare two people", or "what to do with each element" — can be a value you store in a variable, pass to a method, and return from one. That shift is the heart of functional Java.

You've actually met this idea already. Back in module two, you wrote an anonymous class to hand a chunk of behaviour to a method — a whole new-click-listener, with an override of on-click that prints "clicked". All that ceremony wraps just one method's worth of behaviour. A lambda is that same behaviour, written as a value — a function literal. So you write open-paren close-paren, arrow, print "clicked", and pass it straight to add-listener. Same effect, none of the scaffolding. The lambda is "the thing to do on click", passed like any other argument.

What makes this work is the functional interface. Java has no free-floating function type — every value still has a type, and that type is an interface. A lambda's type is a functional interface: an interface with exactly one abstract method. People call that a SAM — single abstract method. And the lambda supplies the body of that one method. So comparator of string, equals, a and b, arrow, a-length minus b-length. Or runnable, equals, empty parens, arrow, print "hi". Comparator has one abstract method, compare; runnable has one, run; so each can be written as a lambda. The compiler matches the lambda's shape to that single method. And this is exactly why the JVM needs no new "function" type — a lambda is just a compact implementation of a one-method interface.

Why is this such a big deal? Because treating behaviour as data collapses a whole category of boilerplate, and unlocks a more declarative style. You can parameterise behaviour, not just values — list dot sort, of by-length, passes the sorting rule as an argument, the same way you'd pass a number. Callbacks become one-liners — "run this when done", or "keep the elements matching this test", handed over inline, with no named class. And small pieces compose — functions can be combined into pipelines, which is section eight, and that's the very foundation of streams, in module seven.

So here are the pillars of this module, in order. First, the lambda syntax itself. Then the even-shorter method reference. Then the standard functional interfaces — function, predicate, consumer, supplier — that name the common shapes, so you rarely declare your own. After that, the deeper mechanics: capturing variables, composing functions, and writing higher-order methods that take and return them. And finally, judgement — when functional style clarifies, and when it obscures. The one idea under all of it: a function is a value. And once behaviour is a value, you can do everything with it that you already do with data.
